this is a production of Cornell  University hi my name is Karen long and  I'm a transfer junior at the school of  integrated plant sciences also known as  sips I'm mostly interested in saying  plant pathology and that interest  started in high school where I took many  agricultural classes including  horticulture and in my horticulture  class two graduate students at the New  York State agriculture Experiment  Station came in with rotting fruit and  vegies and diseased plants and they  taught my class how to identify common  diseases and plants and how to manage  them senior year I had the opportunity  of interning at the Experiment Station  with plant pathologist for a couple of  days and I ran basic tests like gel  electrophoresis I learned how to  sequence DNA and even inoculated beans  with white mold I talked to the plant  pathologists about why doing research is  important and they told me that they do  research to recommend disease management  practices for farmers and they're part  of the solution for food security and  ever since that day I've decided to  pursue a career in plant pathology I've  always wanted to come to Cornell  probably because my agriculture teachers  in alumni and I've done internship  that's the station and the researchers I  worked under are both professors at sips  and they've influenced my decision to  come study here but I went to Community  College first for two years  however my transition from Community  College to CIPS has been really smooth  and I appreciate how the Plant Sciences  program here includes  a wide array of concentrations like  Plant Pathology and the courses within  the concentrations can be very specific  like fungal genomics and as a plant  science student I'm able to take courses  in other concentrations like plant  genetics and breeding and sustainable  plant production also the faculty and  students here at sips are supportive and  collaborative and there's many research  opportunities here on campus so if  you're interested in  science I highly encourage you to see  what sips and Cornell has to offer  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu
 

